export const _animals_bowhead_whale = {"slug":"bowhead-whale","displayName":"Bowhead Whale","icon":"🐋","description":"Discover perfect bowhead whale names! 160+ ideas across 8 categories.","metaDescription":"An Arctic whale that can live over 200 years","maleNames":["Ace","Apollo","Archer","Atlas","Axel","Bandit","Baron","Bear","Beau","Blaze","Bolt","Bruno","Buddy"],"femaleNames":["Abby","Akira","Angel","Annie","Athena","Aurora","Ava","Bailey","Bella","Belle","Birdie","Blossom","Bonnie"],"cuteNames":["Angel","Baby","Bean","Beau","Biscuit","Blossom","Blue","Bonbon","Boo","Bubbles","Buddy","Bumble","Buttercup"],"funnyNames":["Bark Twain","Chewbacca","Nacho","Pickle Rick","Sir Barksalot","Taco","Waffles","Woofgang Puck"],"fantasyNames":["Aerion","Alduin","Aragorn","Arwen","Aslan","Atreyu","Azrael","Balor","Bastion","Celestia"],"uniqueNames":["Aelious","Bodhi","Caelum","Dune","Elowen","Fjord","Gatsby","Havana","Indigo","Juniper"],"coolNames":["Ace","Axel","Bandit","Blade","Blaze","Bolt","Boss","Bullet","Chaos","Cobra"],"babyNames":["Angel","Baby","Bambino","Bean","Bebe","Bitty","Boo","Bud","Button","Chip"],"faq":[{"q":"Good name?","a":"Match personality, easy to say."},{"q":"By appearance?","a":"Definitely!"},{"q":"Human name?","a":"Very popular."},{"q":"How many?","a":"Pick 3-5, test them."},{"q":"Best sounds?","a":"Vowel endings, esp. 'ee'."}],"seoTitle":"Bowhead Whale Name Generator - Bowhead Whale Name Ideas 🐋","seoDescription":"Find perfect bowhead whale names! Browse names across 8 categories. Free!","namingGuide":["Observe first.","Easy to pronounce.","Two syllables.","Test a week.","Avoid commands.","Family input."],"habitat":"Bowhead whales are the only baleen whale that lives exclusively in Arctic and sub-Arctic waters. They inhabit the icy seas around Alaska, Canada, Greenland, and northern Russia, spending winters near the southern edge of pack ice and summers in open leads and polynyas (areas of open water surrounded by ice). Their massive, bow-shaped skull — which gives them their name — can break through ice up to 60 centimeters thick to create breathing holes. This adaptation allows them to survive in frozen environments that would trap other whales.","diet":"Bowhead whales are filter feeders, consuming approximately 2 tons (1,800 kg) of zooplankton daily — primarily copepods, krill, and small crustaceans. They feed by swimming slowly with their enormous mouths open, filtering water through baleen plates that can number up to 360 on each side of the upper jaw. These baleen plates are the longest of any whale, reaching up to 4.5 meters. The bowhead's feeding strategy is remarkably energy-efficient, enabling them to build the thickest blubber layer of any whale species — up to 50 centimeters thick.","behavior":"Bowhead whales are slow swimmers (3-5 km/h normally) but are among the longest-living mammals on Earth, with lifespans exceeding 200 years — a fact proven by the discovery of 19th-century harpoon tips embedded in whales harvested in the 21st century. They are highly vocal, producing a diverse repertoire of songs including complex, jazz-like improvisations that change annually — unique among baleen whales. Bowheads are social, traveling in small pods of 3-5 but congregating in hundreds during migration and feeding. They breach frequently despite their enormous size of up to 20 meters.","facts":"Bowhead whales may have the largest mouth of any animal — up to 5 meters long, 4 meters high, and 2.5 meters wide, roughly one-third of their body length. They lack a dorsal fin, reducing heat loss in frigid Arctic waters. Their blubber can insulate them against water temperatures as low as -2°C. The bowhead genome contains unique adaptations for DNA repair and cancer resistance, explaining their extraordinary longevity. Indigenous Arctic communities have sustainably hunted bowhead whales for thousands of years, and subsistence hunting continues under strict international quotas.","naming":"Bowhead-themed names might reflect their Arctic home, enormous size, or longevity. Consider names like Arctic, Glacier, Polaris, Titan, Frost, Borealis, Tundra, Iceberg, Mammoth, Atlantean, Immortal, Boreas (Greek god of the north wind), Nanuq (polar bear in Inuit — symbolic of Arctic resilience), or Siku (ice in Inuit)."} as const;

