export const _animals_malayan_tiger = {"slug":"malayan-tiger","displayName":"Malayan Tiger","icon":"🐅","description":"Discover perfect malayan tiger names! 160+ ideas across 8 categories.","metaDescription":"Malaysia's national animal fighting for survival","maleNames":["Ace","Apollo","Archer","Atlas","Axel","Bandit","Baron","Bear","Beau","Blaze","Bolt","Buddy","Buster"],"femaleNames":["Abby","Akira","Angel","Annie","Athena","Aurora","Ava","Bailey","Bella","Belle","Birdie","Blossom","Bonnie"],"cuteNames":["Angel","Baby","Bean","Beau","Biscuit","Blossom","Blue","Bonbon","Boo","Bubbles","Buddy","Bumble","Buttercup"],"funnyNames":["Bark Twain","Chewbacca","Nacho","Pickle Rick","Sir Barksalot","Taco","Waffles","Woofgang Puck"],"fantasyNames":["Aerion","Alduin","Aragorn","Arwen","Aslan","Atreyu","Azrael","Balor","Bastion","Celestia"],"uniqueNames":["Aelious","Bodhi","Caelum","Dune","Elowen","Fjord","Gatsby","Havana","Indigo","Juniper"],"coolNames":["Ace","Axel","Bandit","Blade","Blaze","Bolt","Boss","Bullet","Chaos","Cobra"],"babyNames":["Angel","Baby","Bambino","Bean","Bebe","Bitty","Boo","Bud","Button","Chip"],"faq":[{"q":"Good name?","a":"Match personality, easy to say."},{"q":"By appearance?","a":"Definitely!"},{"q":"Human name?","a":"Very popular."},{"q":"How many?","a":"Pick 3-5, test them."},{"q":"Best sounds?","a":"Vowel endings, esp. 'ee'."}],"seoTitle":"Malayan Tiger Name Generator - Malayan Tiger Name Ideas 🐅","seoDescription":"Find perfect malayan tiger names! Browse names across 8 categories. Free!","namingGuide":["Observe first.","Easy to pronounce.","Two syllables.","Test a week.","Avoid commands.","Family input."],"habitat":"Malayan tigers are found exclusively in the southern and central parts of the Malay Peninsula, inhabiting tropical broadleaf evergreen forests, secondary forests, and abandoned agricultural lands. Their range covers the forested areas of Peninsular Malaysia and the southernmost tip of Thailand. The habitat is characterized by high temperatures year-round, monsoon seasons, and dense, multi-layered forest canopies. Forest fragmentation from development has isolated many tiger populations.","diet":"Malayan tigers prey on sambar deer, barking deer, wild boar, bearded pigs, serow, sun bears, and occasionally young elephants and rhinos. As apex predators, they play a crucial role in maintaining the ecological balance of their forest ecosystem. They hunt primarily at dusk and dawn, using stealth and explosive bursts of speed over short distances. A tiger may travel 10-20 kilometers in a single night while hunting.","behavior":"Like all tigers, Malayan tigers are solitary outside of mating season and fiercely territorial. They mark territory with urine, feces, and scratch marks on trees. Males defend larger territories that overlap those of several females. Communication includes vocalizations such as roars (audible up to three kilometers), chuffing (a friendly greeting), and various body postures and facial expressions. They are powerful swimmers and frequently bathe to cool off in their hot, humid environment.","facts":"The Malayan tiger was only classified as a distinct subspecies in 2004, making it one of the newest recognized tiger subspecies. There are fewer than 150 breeding adults remaining in the wild, classifying them as critically endangered. They are culturally significant in Malaysia, appearing on the national coat of arms. Malayan tigers typically have slightly darker orange coats than Indochinese tigers, suited to the dense tropical forest environment.","naming":"Malaysian-inspired tiger names might draw from the Malay language and culture. Consider names like Harimau (tiger), Rimau (from Harimau), Gagah (mighty), Perkasa (powerful), Bayu (wind), Suria (sun), or Santai (calm — an ironic but endearing name for a powerful cat)."} as const;

